.TH SETJMP 3
.SH NAME
setjmp, longjmp \- non-local goto
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <setjmp.h>
.PP
.B int setjmp(jmp_buf env);
.PP
.B void longjmp(jmp_buf env, int val);
.SH DESCRIPTION
These routines are useful for dealing with errors
and interrupts encountered in
a low-level subroutine of a program.
.PP
.I Setjmp
saves its stack environment in
.I env
for later use by
.I longjmp.
It returns value 0.
.PP
.I Longjmp
restores the environment saved by the last call of
.IR setjmp .
It then returns in such a way that execution
continues as if the call of
.I setjmp
had just returned the value
.I val
to the function that invoked
.I setjmp,
which must not itself have returned in the interim.
All accessible data have values as of the time
.I longjmp
was called.
.PP
.B Note:
.I longjmp
is deliberately
.I not
declared
.BR _Noreturn ,
which is <setjmp.h>'s one departure from C11 sec 7.13.2.1.  Declaring it so would
turn every call site into a tail jump for no gain here, and no caller expects it
back in any case.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
signal(2)
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/setjmp.s, include/setjmp.h
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS Written from the calling convention, not ported
v7's is x86 assembly in the port these sources came from, and the c-compiler's
library has none at all.  It is the same problem the kernel's context switch
solves one level down, minus everything that made that hard: no u-area to copy,
no address space to swap, no interrupts to mask.
.SS The buffer
.B jmp_buf
is
.BR "int[8]" ,
as on every v7 machine, but what goes into it is this machine's, and only
setjmp.s may touch it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
0     r13         the return address into setjmp's caller
1     r15         the stack pointer at the call
2,3,4 r5,r6,r7    the callee-saved registers compiled code uses
5     R           the AU mode register
6,7   \- unused \-
.fi
.RE
.PP
It is
.B int
and not
.BR char ,
so a
.B jmp_buf
is a plain word address rather than a fat pointer and goes straight into an index
register.
.SS Only r5..r7 of the callee-saved set
The convention says r1..r7 must be preserved, but the compiler-support prologue
saves exactly r5, r6 and r7, and compiled code allocates nothing below r5 \- what
a disassembly shows is r6 and r7 as the parameter and auto pointers, r8..r12 as
expression scratch, and r13/r14/r15.  A function that clobbered r1..r4 across a
call would already break an ordinary return, so there is nothing here for
.I setjmp
to save that the prologue does not.
.SS Neither routine has a prologue
.I Setjmp
has one parameter, so it arrives in the accumulator, nothing is pushed, and r15
at entry is already the caller's: a callee whose arguments were all in registers
pops nothing.
.I Longjmp
never returns, so its one pushed argument is never popped \- r15 comes from the
buffer.
.PP
.I Longjmp
restores the index registers with
.BR wtc / vtm ,
which is why
.I val
never has to leave the accumulator.
.SS Why R is saved at all
The ABI fixes R = 7 at every call boundary, so the R a
.I longjmp
is entered with is by construction the R its
.I setjmp
is entitled to return with \- the slot ought to be unnecessary.  It is kept
because a program may
.I longjmp out of a signal handler,
and a handler is not a call boundary.  It costs one word.  lib/test/signals ends
on exactly that case:
.IR sleep (3),
whose handler leaves by
.I longjmp
and never returns through the frame at all.
